#!/usr/bin/env perl

package Trog::Bin::SetupProvisioner;

use 5.041;

use strict;
use warnings FATAL => 'all';

use re '/aa';

# Basically our 'install script'

#TODO ideally, we just ship this all as a 'config rpm/deb'.

=head1 NAME

setup_provisioner - prepare this machine to host guests

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    bin/setup_provisioner

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The install script for a I<hypervisor>: everything a machine needs before
anything can build guests on it.

Every step is still a stub: each returns true and says what it ought to be
doing.  Each step's number is what main() returns when that step fails.

=head2 What this is not

The machine that B<runs> the provisioner is a guest like any other, and
L<Provisioner::Recipe::trogrunner> is what builds it: perl, the CPAN
dependencies, F</etc/trog-provisioner>, and a key a hypervisor will trust.  That
is where the checkout, the toolchain and the configuration went.

The rest went the same way, one recipe at a time -- C<auditd>, C<fail2ban>,
C<cron> and C<tcms> are all recipes now, so a management interface or a log
ingester is a guest to build rather than a step to run here.  There used to be
an rsyslog step, which opened a syslog listener by editing F</etc/rsyslog.conf>
on whatever machine you ran this from, while every guest was told to ship its
logs to the hypervisor -- a different machine on any fleet with more than one.
Where a guest sends its logs is L<Provisioner::Recipe::logshipper> now, and the
listener at the other end is L<Provisioner::Recipe::logcollector>.

What is left is the part that cannot be a guest, because it is the thing guests
run on.

=cut

sub main {
    my @args = @_;

    #    GetOptionsFromArray(\@args,
    #
    #    );

    setup_kvm()           or return 1;
    setup_public_bridge() or return 2;
    setup_iso_builder()   or return 3;
    setup_storage_pool()  or return 4;
    setup_apparmor()      or return 5;
    setup_firewall()      or return 6;
    return 0;
}

